* 18 Sep 26 dm - THE INTRINSIC CHOWN NEEDS ROOT, AND THE ADMINISTRATOR IS NEVER
*   ROOT (S.26).  Measured on the first fresh cycle's witness run (A4): every
*   account created after an install came out sdsys:sdusers - six "Unable change
*   ownership of directory ... err: 1" (EPERM) lines - because OS$CHOWN runs as
*   the calling session, and a local sdsys session is not root.  So the
*   privileged step goes through sd-elevate like the setgid, addgroup and
*   groupadd calls above, and the helper re-derives the pair's legality (a
*   person may own only its own account directory, through its own sdu_ group)
*   rather than trusting this program.  THE INTRINSIC STAYS FOR ROOT: the
*   install bootstrap (the IS_INSTALL CPROC's seeding) runs as root and an
*   adopted account's user is not one the helper's whitelist would accept.
   if system(27) = 0 then
      chown_parm = uid:@VM:gid:@VM:set.owner.path
      * crt 'set.owner: ':chown_parm
      result = ospath(chown_parm,OS$CHOWN)
   end else
      result = 0
      os.execute "sudo /usr/local/sbin/sd-elevate chown-account " : uid : " " : gid : " " : set.owner.path
      if OS.ERROR() = 0 then result = 1
   end
   if result <= 0 then
     st = set.owner.path:' err: ':status()
     display sysmsg(10000,st) ;* Unable change ownership of directory error %1 
   end

